2.08 (2026-11-12):
	Ornaments (the manual's new section "Ornaments"; provisional):
	lines, polygons, rectangles and ovals drawn over or under the
	text, whose coordinates are reference points computed from the
	places where characters are drawn (the anchors of the box of a
	character or of a range, their weighted means, offsets, the x
	of one point with the y of another, minima and maxima), computed
	anew at every redisplay; with marks in their indices they follow
	the text.  Subcommand "ornament" (create, delete, names,
	configure, cget, points, coords, point).  Test t/ornament.t.
	Private marks: an index of an ornament that names no mark and no
	tag becomes a mark of the ornament's own, invisible to the
	program (mark names/next/previous, dump, block list; names
	starting with \001 are reserved), so that the point stays on its
	character; the wrappers [leftward => INDEX] and [rightward =>
	INDEX] make one of a given gravity.  An ornament all of whose
	indices are private marks is deleted with its text (option
	-keep prevents it); undo does not bring it back.  The stand-in
	of a position that is not displayed takes the x the position had
	when last displayed (else the middle of the text area, or with
	the new widget option -ornamentproportional the place
	proportional to its offset in its line): no line is laid out for
	it any more.
	New points [line => I1, I2, N, ANCHOR] (the piece of a range on
	its N-th display line) and [outline => I1, I2] (all the corners
	of a range's outline, in a line or a polygon); anchors mw, m, me
	(the mid line) and [FX, FY] (fractions of the box).
	Types arc (-start, -extent, -style as on a canvas) and text
	(-text, -font, -anchor); distances of an offset in em and ex of
	the font of its point (emmin, emmid, emmax, exmin, exmid, exmax
	for the fonts of a range); the point [yx => P1, P2].
	"mark next" skipped a mark at the very start of the first leaf of
	a block; "mark previous" missed the marks in the leaves of a block
	before the index but the first, and those after the block on its
	line (BUGS 35).
	A layout callback may return ornaments of the block among its
	lists: [TYPE, X1, Y1, ..., -option => value, ...], in pixels
	from the top left corner of the block, with the options of
	"ornament create"; drawn with the block every time it is drawn,
	under its text (among the stolen lines, in the order returned)
	or over it; not leaves; their options are parsed once per
	layout and kept with it.  A bad one is a background error, as
	other bad results.  (Manual: "Layout callback", "Ornaments of
	blocks".)
	Templates on a block type: "block configure TYPE -ornaments
	LIST", ornaments drawn for every displayed block of the type,
	whose points are computed from the boxes of the block and of its
	leaves with the new points [leaf => K, ANCHOR] and [block =>
	ANCHOR] (and mix, offset, xy, yx, min, max of them; no indices).
	A template naming a leaf that is missing or not shown is not
	drawn for that block.  "block cget TYPE -ornaments" gives the
	list.
	Demo: the strokes are ornaments instead of stolen lines (block
	addline still works, for the LR delimiters and the backgrounds):
	the Radical's sign is a broken line its layout callback returns,
	that grows with the contents (a tick, a stroke down to the bottom,
	up to the top left of the contents, the bar over them) instead of
	a glyph with a vertical line added for tall contents; the
	Fraction's bar is a template (-ornaments of the type); the rules
	of Table and SpanTable and the lines of Std are black rectangles
	returned by their callbacks.  The tests that looked at the
	stolen lines of layout results look at the ornaments.
	Demo: a menu "Ornaments": "Box under selection" (a light filled
	rectangle under the selected text), "Arrow from mark" (the first
	use remembers the start of the selection in the mark ornArrowFrom,
	the second draws an arched red arrow from there to the selection),
	"Brace under selection" (an underbrace, a smoothed line sized in
	em), "Remove all".  Their indices are the selection's ends,
	private marks of the ornaments.  Test t/demo-ornaments.t.
	A smoothed ornament that is straight on a half pixel no longer
	jitters between two rows of pixels (rounding).

2.07 (2026-11-12):
	"bbox" of a position inside a leaf of a block that has been drawn
	was off downwards by the top of the block in the window (BUGS 32).
	"bbox" of the tail of a block at the end of a display line reached
	to the right edge of the window, not just over its last leaf
	(BUGS 33).
	A mouse position beyond the end of a wrapped display line ending
	with a multi-byte character ("@x,y", the mark "current") was inside
	the character; a mark set there split it into NUL bytes (BUGS 34).
	Demo: two block types whose layout callbacks take the tree as it
	is: OpLimits, a big operator with limits R( op | Lower( l1 | l2 )
	| upper | index ) (a ragged tree; flattened, empty, missing and
	extra parts laid out too), and SpanTable, a table whose cells span
	columns and rows by their values ({ cols => 2, rows => 3 } or
	"2x3"; HTML's rule for the slots covered; the columns and rows a
	cell does not fit in grow; rules between the cells).  The demo's
	blockInsert writes down any tree (Tk::Text::Node), values
	(Tk::Text::Valued), instance data and tagged strings.  Tests
	t/demo-oplimits.t, t/demo-table.t; t/display-random.t lays them
	out after random edits (ETEXT_DISPLAY_RANDOM for more seeds).
	Demo: blocks re-created by File/Open (save.tsf) and Edit/Int Paste
	get their instance data and node values back (a SpanTable kept
	no spans).

2.06 (2026-11-11):
	Ragged trees: the leaves of a block's tree may be at different
	depths (the manual's new section "Ragged trees").  The widget
	keeps the stored tree uniform, a shallower leaf hanging from its
	parent through padding nodes (a flag in the node), which no
	command names; every command that names nodes follows the real
	tree: block at and block of (a node is a list of its children,
	leaves and nodes side by side), block nodedata (levels count real
	ancestors from the leaf; negative levels from the root), the
	addresses of values in undo steps and in -valuecommand's $where
	(where the node's first leaf starts, the level counted from that
	leaf), block trim, and the layout callback's arguments (with
	-layoutdepth, a shallower leaf is presented inside lists of one
	element down to that depth).  The uniform tree shows only in
	block list and in the levels of block split.
	New command "block sprout index1 ?index2?": with two indices the
	leaf holding the range becomes three children of its parent, the
	text before the range, a new node holding the range as its only
	leaf, and the text after it (a range that starts on the delimiter
	before the leaf, or ends just after the one after it, makes no
	start or end piece); with one index (decision O8) the leaf the
	index names gets a new node above it, the only leaf of a block
	included.  Returns the number of separators put in.
	New command "block flatten index level": the node "level" real
	levels above the leaf of index becomes one leaf; at the root the
	block becomes a single leaf (block at gives one number, block list
	the depth -1, the layout callback gets the leaf alone, nodedata 0
	and -1 name it; a newline, a split or block sprout give it a root
	again).  Returns the number of separators deleted.
	Deletions that join two leaves of different depths first give the
	shallower one new nodes (WRAPs) until both are equally deep, then
	join them as before; a newline typed in a leaf, and block split at
	level 0, make a sibling at the leaf's depth.
	Format change of block list (decision L1): the separator before a
	leaf with padding is a pair [depth, defect] (a blessed array of
	class Tk::Text::BlockSeparator, the defect being the number of
	padding nodes above the leaf), and a padded first leaf gets
	[undef, defect] in front of it; the list of a tree whose leaves are
	equally deep is as before.  A single-leaf block has the depth -1.
	The undo and the redo of every structural edit are exact for
	ragged trees: the tree, the padding and the values come back (the
	undo is the inverse sequence of the edit's steps; its lists carry
	the padding).  block sprout and block flatten are recorded for
	undo, each a kind of edit of its own for -autoseparators.
	-valuecommand: the steps of block sprout, block flatten and of
	deletions across depths are told one by one (a WRAP or a PULL OUT
	changes the level in the address of the nodes above: "move" with
	the place after the step); new event "copy" for the value block
	sprout shares between the pieces; new event "new" (decision O5,
	option A), told before a change for each node or leaf it creates
	without a value, with its place after the change and the value
	undef (newlines in blocks, block split, block insert, block sprout,
	the WRAPs of a deletion, the leaves a dissolved block leaves in the
	block around it, a single-leaf block's new root); not told for what
	an undo or a redo re-creates.  block split now tells "move" for
	the root's value when it adds levels: it inserts the new nodes
	between the root and its children, so the root's address grows.
	The error of block nodedata for a level beyond the root says
	"... has N levels above this leaf" (it said "above its leaves").
	PROVISIONAL (BUGS 28): which sequence of steps an edit uses (block
	flatten's recipe, a deletion's WRAPs of the shallower leaf), the
	value rules of the steps (a join keeps the start side's value; a
	pull out the node's; block sprout shares the former leaf's value),
	and the -valuecommand events of these commands ("copy", "new", the
	steps told one by one).
	Fixed (BUGS 29, since 2.01): the undo of a deletion inside a block
	re-created the separators of level above 0 with the tags of the
	place, also those the deleted range had off there.
	Fixed (BUGS 30, since 2.05): -getflags was ORed into the widget's
	own text extractions, so reserved bits changed what block list and
	the undo's records contained (0x200 left the node values out).
	The widget now uses only the documented bits and ignores the
	others.
	Fixed (BUGS 31, since 2.05): a value set, after a deletion that
	dissolved a nested block, on a leaf the dissolution made in the
	enclosing block went, with the undo, to the leaf of the re-created
	block at the same place, and the redo, which dissolves that block
	again, took it into the undo step with the block's values: the
	leaf came back without it.  Such a value now waits in the undo
	step (told "stash"), as one whose place does not exist after the
	undo does (BUGS 26), and the redo gives it back to the leaf of the
	enclosing block (told "restore").  The random -valuecommand test
	no longer lets an undo or a redo leave a node without a value.
	Tests: t/ragged.t, t/flatten.t, t/getflags.t; t/stress.t and
	t/valuecommand-random.t edit ragged trees at random, undo and redo
	at once and compare the block list with values and the shape of
	every block; the random -valuecommand test checks in half of its
	widgets, where every node has a value before each command, that
	every node a command creates is told "new".

2.05 (2026-11-10):
	The values the widget keeps for Perl (on the nodes and leaves of
	blocks, marks, runs of tags, and as the instance data of blocks)
	live in data containers (tkTextData.h): the Perl value is to
	become one part of what is attached there, the rest holding, for
	example, switches for the operations that now follow one fixed
	rule (how the data of two merged tag runs is combined).  All code
	goes through the operations of the header; the merge policy (N5,
	the first container present wins) is one function,
	TkTextDataMerge.  Two representations, chosen when building: A
	(the default) is the Perl value itself and compiles to what the
	code did before; B ("ETEXT_DATA_ARRAYS=1 perl Makefile.PL", adds
	-DTK_TEXT_DATA_ARRAYS=1) is one pointer to a block of typed slots
	(a size, a capacity, an array of slots and an array of unsigned
	char type codes), of which the Perl value is the slot of type
	TK_TEXT_DATA_LANG.  In both the field is one pointer wide.  Undo
	records, undo atoms and the list of values to free carry whole
	containers.  The Perl interface (block nodedata, mark data, tag
	data, block data, the [name, value] elements of block list, dump
	-values) reads and writes only the Perl value, as before: setting
	undef removes only that part.  Limits of B, documented in the
	header: block list carries only the Perl values, so the other
	slots do not follow text re-inserted from a list, and the instance
	data of a block deleted and brought back by undo comes back from
	the list.  A widget built with B has a hidden subcommand for the
	tests (_dataslot), and t/data-slots.t (skipped with A) checks that
	a slot survives drop, undo, redo and merges and that each
	container is freed once.  Nothing changes for Perl code: the test
	suite gives the same results with A as 2.04, and passes with B.
	BUGS 22 and 23, found on the way: the instance data of a block is
	freed at once, not when the widget command ends, when "block data"
	replaces it and when an undo step that listed it is discarded.
	demo/etext.pl starts with its "#!/usr/bin/perl" line again: two
	stray lines, a copy of two lines of its body, came before it.
	Fixed: deleting a block of a type with -empty => 1 unlinked its
	segment and never freed it (72 bytes per block, since the 1997
	code): t/stress.t lost 349 such pieces under valgrind, now none.
	t/leak.t watches the process size over 50000 such deletions.
	Fixed (BUGS 22): the instance data that "block data" replaces, or
	removes with undef, is freed when the widget command ends, as every
	other value, and no longer at once inside it: its DESTROY runs under
	the stopgap (commands that would modify the widget are refused),
	still before blockData returns.
	Fixed (BUGS 23): the undo of a deletion keeps the instance data of
	the blocks it deletes or dissolves as a record of its own (addressed
	by the block's head), as it keeps node and run values, and no longer
	as a copy in its block list, which now leaves instance data out.  The
	undo gives the whole container back to the block it re-creates (with
	representation B, its other slots too), the redo takes it again, and
	a discarded step (edit reset, -maxundo, a new change after an undo)
	frees it when the widget command ends, under the stopgap.  The same
	for the redo of an insertion, which re-inserts a block list.
	Setting block data now counts as setting a value for the snapshot
	an undoable deletion takes (BUGS 13).
	Fixed (BUGS 20): "tag names" at the head of a block reports the tags
	in effect there, as "tag ranges", "dump" and the searches do; it
	left out a tag that switches off inside the block.  The display, the
	elide test and the mouse bindings keep the rule of 1997: such a tag
	does not style the block as a whole.  The manual's "the behaviour of
	tags on the start of block is undefined" says so instead.
	Fixed: a tag with -elide on that started before a block and ended
	inside it hid the whole block and the rest of the line, text it does
	not cover included; the two loops of the display that skip hidden
	text now stop at a block inside which such a tag switches, and the
	block draws what the tag does not cover (BUGS 24).
	The manual ("Node values and editing") says what a deletion does to
	the last of a block's nodes directly above the leaves (and to its
	ancestors below the root): they hold the block's tail, so they are
	never deleted whole; when all their leaves are deleted they are
	joined to the node before them, and their values follow the rule for
	joined nodes (moved if that node has none, dropped otherwise).  It
	said that such a node was deleted with its value.  t/values.t pins it.
	Fixed (BUGS 18): the undo of a block insert whose range crossed
	nodes of an enclosing block gives the nodes back exactly, with their
	values.  The block insert flattens the separators of its range (the
	leaves become children of the new block's single node), and the undo
	dissolved the block at level 0; the undo step now records the level
	of each separator in the range and of the one after it, and the undo
	sets each back to its level after dissolving the block (splitting or
	joining nodes; no text moves), before the values come back to the
	nodes at their addresses.  The redo stays exact.  The review's random
	undo/redo exactness check, which failed on 11 of 300 runs (all this
	bug), passes on all of them.
	t/demo.t hides its probe MainWindow instead of destroying it, and
	destroys it last: Perl/Tk's option-lookup cache goes stale when a
	MainWindow is destroyed, and the demo's MainWindow, created after
	it, crashed now and then (BUGS 21, under Wine).
	Fixed (BUGS 25): an -empty block that had moved to another line (a
	newline or a block split before it, block insert nesting it) kept
	pointing to its old line; "block at" on its head crashed.  The
	B-tree check under "debug" looks for it.
	Fixed (BUGS 26): the undo of a deletion that dissolved a block freed
	a value set after the deletion on a leaf made of what was left of
	the block; it waits in the undo step now, and the redo gives it
	back.
	New option -valuecommand (BUGS 17): a callback told, before a
	command changes anything, which values the change will detach and
	where they are, while the widget is unchanged: $cb->($widget,
	$event, $kind, $where, $value, $cause).  This first step covers
	setting and unsetting values (block nodedata, tag data, mark data,
	block data, mark unset), the runs that tag add, tag remove and tag
	delete drop or merge, the values an undo or a redo gives back
	("restore") or moves aside, and those of undo steps thrown away
	("discard", at the end of the command).  A detach that was not
	foreseen is reported "late" after the change, and under "debug" the
	command fails.  While the callback runs, the widget refuses the
	commands that would change it.  t/valuecommand.t.
	The look-ahead of -valuecommand covers the structural changes too:
	deletions (leaves and nodes deleted or joined, as the manual's
	rules say, blocks deleted or dissolved with all their values and
	instance data, runs gone or merged, also around the heads and tails
	of dissolved blocks), block insert (the two parts of the cut leaves
	joined, nodes left without leaves), block trim, and the joins of
	nodes by which the undo of a block insert sets the separator levels
	back.
	t/valuecommand-random.t runs random commands (undo and redo
	included) on widgets with nested blocks and values everywhere,
	with "debug" on, and checks that every value detached was told
	before, at a place that names it during the call, that every value
	freed was told drop or discard, and that no late event occurs.  The
	manual documents the option ("Telling the program: -valuecommand").
	The stopgap of 2.04 is gone (BUGS 17): a DESTROY method run while
	the values a command dropped are freed may change the widget again.
	Such a change is an undo step of its own; the values it drops are
	freed in the same pass; a chain of such changes is refused 10 deep.
	Only the -valuecommand callback is refused changes (same list of
	commands as the stopgap had).
	A ready-made -valuecommand: -valuecommand => 'DETACH' calls the
	method DETACH of the widget (in eText.pm), which calls
	$value->DETACH($widget, $event, $kind, $where, $cause) for a value
	that is an object with such a method, and skips the others.
	The 10 rounds of values freed in which DESTROY methods may change
	the widget are a limit that the program can raise (the owner's
	design): when a change from a DESTROY method is about to be made
	at the limit, the widget calls $object->DESTROYmult($n, $limit)
	on the object whose DESTROY method makes it ($n counts the
	requests of the freeing pass from 1, $limit is the current limit,
	10 at first), and a positive integer $more raises the limit to
	$limit + $more; anything else, no such method, a non-object, or a
	method that dies (a background error) and the change is refused
	as before, the error naming the limit.  Asked is the innermost
	DESTROY running, so an object held inside a value answers for its
	own change.  The limit and the count reset with each outermost
	command.  t/destroymult.t.
	The manual says what happens when Perl code the widget calls dies
	("When Perl code called by the widget dies"), and t/dying.t checks
	it: a DESTROY method that dies, or that lets a refused change
	leave it, gives Perl's "(in cleanup)" warning (only where warnings
	are on: the refusal comes from a Perl/Tk method compiled without
	"use warnings", so it is warned only under perl -w, and an eval in
	DESTROY catches it), and the values and the command go on; a
	-valuecommand callback, a DETACH method or DESTROYmult that dies
	is a background error and the command goes on.  Each value is
	freed once in every case, and nothing the callback got is kept.
	Postponed (BUGS 27): -valuecommand tells before the command
	changes anything only for commands that make one change; those
	that make several (edit undo and redo, insert with tag lists, tag
	add and remove over several ranges, a deletion through the end of
	the text) tell before each of their changes, and a value an undo
	or a redo displaces is told just before it moves.  The manual's
	-valuecommand section says so in a note marked "Postponed".

2.04 (2026-11-09):
	Undo records block insert, block split, block trim, tag add and
	tag remove (BUGS 12 and 16), each reversed exactly: the undo of a
	split deletes its separator (and takes away the levels it added
	above the root), that of a block insert dissolves the block by
	deleting its head (the leaves it moved whole get their values
	back, its instance data and own values wait in the step for the
	redo), that of a trim adds the root again, those of tag add and
	remove give the tag in the range exactly the ranges it had; the
	redo does the change again.  What they drop (merge losers, emptied
	nodes, a trimmed root's value, removed runs) goes into the undo
	step, as for deletions.  Each is an edit kind of its own for
	-autoseparators (Tk's rule: a change of kind starts a step).
	"sel" is not recorded, nor a tag change that changes nothing.
	These changes now count for "edit modified".  Not exact: the undo
	of a block insert whose range crossed nodes of an enclosing block
	does not re-create the node boundaries (BUGS 18).  A deletion
	through the end of the text now records the tags it takes off the
	final newline, and its undo puts them back.  "block split" on the
	last (dummy) line splits at the end of the line before it.  New
	test: t/undo-block.t; t/stress.t runs all of it at random.
	dump -values: every entry gets a fourth field, the value of a mark
	(mark data) or of the run a tagon starts (tag data), undef for the
	other entries and for marks and runs without a value, so that the
	list stays one of entries of one length; a -command callback gets
	it as a fourth argument.  Not part of -all; alone it means -all
	-values.  Node values and block instance data are not dumped
	(block list shows them).  Without -values, dump is unchanged.
	Fixed: the undo of a deletion gave the value of a run starting
	where the deletion started to a run of the same tag re-created
	inside the restored text (the text is re-inserted piece by piece,
	and the piece joined the run for a moment), and the redo then
	lost the value; release 2.03 too.
	A stopgap: while the values a command dropped are being freed (a
	DESTROY method runs), the commands that would modify the widget
	fail with "cannot modify .t while its values are being freed";
	reading commands and those that only change the view work.  The
	manual lists the commands refused (section "When values are
	freed"); BUGS 17 says what the real fix is to be.  New test:
	t/freeing.t.
	The manual describes block insert exactly: the ranges accepted,
	a range in a block crossing leaves and nodes, the leaves moved
	becoming children of the new block's single node (the structure
	above them flattened, their separators of depth 0), and what is
	left in the enclosing block.
	block list: a mark with a value (mark data) is listed as
	MarkLeft or MarkRight holding [name, value], as a valued run is
	listed as TagOn [name, value]; a mark without a value is listed as
	before.  Inserting a list back (the undo's path) gives a mark the
	value of such an element; the undo's own lists leave mark values
	out, as they leave node and run values out.  The demo reads mark
	elements in both forms (Tk::Text::MarkLeft::name, MarkRight::name).
	The redo of an insertion re-inserts it exactly: undoing an
	insertion records the inserted range in block-list form (as a
	deletion records what it deletes), and the redo inserts that list
	instead of the plain string (Tk 8.4's way).  The text comes back
	with the tags it was inserted with, the separators it made in a
	block and the marks in it, and its runs get their values back,
	which the redo used to free (BUGS 11).
	Fixed: a backward tag search inside a block whose root has nodes
	two levels deep (tag prevrange, finding the run of a tag value,
	and through these an undo) walked off the end of a list of nodes
	and crashed (FindTagEnd); release 2.03 too.
	Fixed: "block list" of a block whose only leaf is empty left out
	the marks and tag toggles in that leaf, so the undo of a deletion
	over such a block did not put them back (a mark stayed where the
	deletion had moved it, a tag ended on the wrong side of the
	block); release 2.03 too.
	Fixed: the redo of a block insert re-created the block even when
	its type had been made -empty since the undo, over contents (the
	command refuses that), and the display then crashed; such a redo
	does nothing now, as one whose block type was deleted.
	Fixed: the redo of a block trim did not look again whether the
	root had a value, so a root value set or unset between the undo
	and the redo put the values on the wrong nodes after redo and
	undo.
	Fixed: "tag add b end" (an empty range) made an undo step that
	did nothing, and set the modified flag; it records nothing now.
	Fixed: with -undo off, tag add and tag remove did not set the
	modified flag (insertions and block changes do, and the manual
	says they count); they do now, but for "sel" and changes that
	change nothing.
	While values are being freed, the rule is now the same for tags
	and block types: a query of tag configure, tag bind or block
	configure works if the tag or type exists, and is refused if it
	would create one (tag configure and tag bind queries used to
	create the tag, while every block configure was refused).
	Fixed: a block insert over several lines whose second or later
	line was the first one on display left the view pointing into a
	line moved into the block, and the next redisplay crashed; the
	undo and redo of block changes reached it through "see".  The
	view now goes to the first line of the range; release 2.03 too.
	The same stale view made the undo of a deletion abort ("got index
	in internal BTree") and a redisplay abort ("SplitSeg reached end
	of line!") after other block inserts; gone with it.
	Fixed: in a block whose tree has inner nodes, a tag search did
	not stop at its end when that end was in a node of the block
	without a toggle of the tag, and the first toggle of a tag in
	a block was placed one position (or a skipped node) too far
	left; so "tag remove" over a range crossing a node boundary
	removed the tag far beyond the range, and the undo of a tag add
	lost the ranges the tag had (release 2.03 too).
	Fixed: a deletion that the rule of the final newline makes empty
	(only that newline was named) was recorded as an undo step that
	did nothing, and set the modified flag; it records and modifies
	nothing now, unless the newline had tags, which it takes off as
	before (that is recorded); release 2.03 too.
	Fixed: a block insert over lines in different nodes of the text's
	B-tree (from about a dozen lines on) left the tree inconsistent
	(lines linked into the wrong node, line counts taken off the wrong
	node, a node left with too few lines); the B-tree check (debug 1)
	aborted; release 2.03 too.
	Fixed: a block insert that emptied the B-tree node of its last
	line left that node's parent with too few children (the B-tree
	check aborted); the first ancestor left is rebalanced now.
	Fixed: the undo of a deletion lost the start of a tag that began
	inside a block of the deleted text and went on after it, when
	another tag was switched on and off later in the text (a
	temporary mark name was used twice); release 2.03 too.
	Fixed: the undo of a deletion of a block between two runs of a tag
	(which the deletion joined) gave the re-created block's head and
	tail the tag; release 2.03 too.
	Fixed: the undo of a deletion that dissolved a block and joined a
	run with a value in it to a later run of the tag put the value on
	the later run; release 2.03 too.
	Fixed: a deletion of nothing but the final newline that has only
	the selection on it set the modified flag (sel is not content).
	With -undo off, tag add and tag remove decide whether they modify
	from the state at the start of the range and the first toggle in
	it (no list of ranges is made any more).
	Fixed: the undo of a deletion re-created a block whose contents
	switch a tag off with its tail still carrying the tag (as at the
	place the block was made); release 2.03 too.

2.03 (2026-11-08):
	Values: a Perl scalar can be kept with every node of the tree of
	a block, the leaves included ("block nodedata index level
	?value?", blockNodedata), with every mark ("mark data name
	?value?", markData) and with every run of a tag ("tag data
	tagName index ?value?", tagData).  The widget keeps a copy, and
	the values move with the text: where an edit joins nodes or
	merges runs the first value present wins, block split and a run
	cut in two leave the new part without one, a mark keeps its value
	through every move.  A value is freed only when it is replaced,
	when its node, mark or run goes (or loses a join or a merge) and
	when the widget is destroyed, and then only when the widget
	command returns, the change finished, so that a DESTROY method may
	use the widget; the instance data of a block (block data) is now
	freed the same way.  With -undo, a deletion keeps the values it
	drops in its undo step and the undo gives them back; edit reset,
	-maxundo, a new edit dropping the redo history and the widget's
	destruction free them.  "block list" writes the node values of a
	block as a fourth element of its header, and the start of a run
	with a value as a TagOn holding [name, value] instead of a
	reference to the name (a program that reads TagOn as a scalar
	reference has to accept both; the demo's TeX export now does).
	The manual has a section "Values"; the known limits are in BUGS.
	New tests: t/values.t, t/values-marks.t, t/values-runs.t.
	Fixes found on the way: block insert inside a block, over a range
	spanning nodes of the block's tree, corrupted the tree (negative
	line counts, lines linked across nodes), and "get" then crashed;
	undo re-created the tags of a deletion inexactly (text put back
	inside a tagged range took the tag, as inserted text does, though
	it had not had it); undo re-created a block whose root has a
	single child one level too shallow (the depth in the header was
	ignored); a block dissolved by a deletion of its head or tail
	leaked its first internal line and its dummy line (about 140 bytes
	each time); eTextSyms.h was stale and left four symbols without
	the prefix; the manual gave 1 as the depth of a separator between
	leaves of the same parent, where it is 0; the message for a wrong
	block subcommand did not name "data".
	Crashes of block editing, found by random sequences of block
	editing and undo (all of them in 2.02 too; t/stress.t has a
	minimal script for each and a bounded random run): block split
	did not tell the display about its newline, which then read past
	the end of the line it had cut short (a segfault, typically in
	the undo that followed); block insert of an -empty block at the
	start of a line dereferenced NULL; block insert with its end
	before its start cut the text apart (now an error); deleting an
	-empty block inside a block took it off the block's length twice
	(the block lost its last character, and later panicked); a
	deletion inside a block that joined leaves of different nodes
	lost the tag counts of the lines it moved, and in the deletions
	that dissolve a block left a node without children (a segfault);
	a node merge while a block's line was changing counted the block's
	tags twice and left the tag's root on a freed node (a segfault);
	"block list", and so the undo record of a deletion, from just after
	a block reported an empty piece of it (the undo put a stray block
	in); the undo of a deletion could re-create a block on the last,
	dummy, line; an undefined index segfaulted (the parser wrote into
	the argument's string; it works on a copy now, as Perl/Tk's text
	does); with the B-tree check on, a deletion from the tail of a
	nested block panicked ("segment has size <= 0").
	Undo: after "edit undo", "edit redo" or "edit reset" the next edit
	starts an undo step of its own; a deletion after the undo of a
	deletion used to join the step of the insertion before it, so a
	second undo took that insertion back as well (in 2.02 too).
2.02 (2026-11-07):
	Scrolling copies the visible lines with TkScrollWindow and redraws
	the damaged region, as Tk 8.4 does on every platform.  The tk4.0
	code used XCopyArea and then waited with XWindowEvent for
	GraphicsExpose/NoExpose; on Windows XWindowEvent is an empty stub in
	Perl/Tk, so the first scroll would have read an event that was
	never filled in (seen under Wine: 2.01 stops at the first scroll
	with "DisplayText received unknown exposure event").  Makefile.PL
	links -lX11 only outside Windows, and compiles with -Wall under gcc
	and clang (what it found unused is gone).  Windows: built and
	tested under Wine with Strawberry Perl 5.38.2.2 and Perl/Tk 804.036
	built there; all tests pass, given the Perl/Tk fixes in the
	repository's upstream/ (without them Perl/Tk 804.036 does not build
	with current mingw-w64, or crashes at its first update).  The
	display line tests name a monospaced font (Courier) instead of
	relying on the default, which is Courier New on Windows.
2.01 (2026-11-07):
	The first release of the modernized distribution (the 2.00 entry
	below, the development history of the port, was never released
	under that number).  The release layout: the user documentation
	(README.md, Changes, BUGS, doc/etext.pod) is inside the
	distribution; Makefile.PL declares the prerequisites (Perl 5.10,
	Tk 804.027, Test::More) and the metadata (CPAN META spec 2:
	license, repository, bug tracker; the bundled FreezeThaw and
	Text::TeX under utils/ are not indexed); the Tcl/Tk flavour of the
	sources is not shipped (it stays in the repository).  Since the
	2.00 entry was written: undo re-creates embedded images and
	windows; embedded windows inside blocks can be addressed by name
	and index; "display linestart", "display lineend" and "+N display
	lines", with Home and End on the display line (all described
	under 2.00 below).  The reports for upstream (Perl/Tk, Tcl/Tk) are
	in upstream/reports/ of the repository, one file per report.
2.00 (2026-11, not released):
	The Perl extension (Tk::eText) made to build and work with
	today's Perl (5.38) and Perl/Tk (804.036):
	- standalone ExtUtils::MakeMaker/Tk::MMutil build against the
	  installed Perl/Tk, no Tk build tree needed; loaded with
	  XSLoader; all internal symbols prefixed (eTextSyms.h) so that
	  they cannot clash with Tk's own text widget;
	- Tk-b9 "Lang" layer replaced by the Tk 804 API (Tcl_Obj
	  arguments and results, Tcl_ListObjGetElements, Perl regexps
	  via Tcl_GetRegExpFromObj, Tcl_ObjSetVar2 for -count);
	- XFontStruct/TkMeasureChars/TkDisplayChars replaced by Tk_Font,
	  Tk_MeasureChars, Tk_DrawChars, Tk_UnderlineChars, with tab and
	  newline handling done by the widget, as in Tk 8.4;
	- Tk::eText is a subclass of Tk::Text and gets all its bindings;
	  the demo moved to demo/etext.pl and ported (Tk 804 has no
	  Menubar with a command() method; option names need dashes;
	  ornaments are Unicode characters now);
	- automated tests in t/ (t/basic.t, t/leak.t).
	Memory leaks fixed:
	- each call of the block layout callback leaked a reference to
	  the widget (hence the widget's Perl object could never be
	  freed); the cursor blink re-lays-out the line it is on, so
	  with the cursor in a block this leaked twice a second;
	- TkTextRedrawRegion leaked an X region per call (focus change,
	  expose);
	- "block list" leaked one scalar per call, and every typed
	  element of the result leaked its payload;
	- the embedded-window element of "block list" leaked a widget
	  reference.
	Other fixes:
	- search -nocase works for exact (non-regexp) searches (the
	  lower-casing of the pattern had been disabled);
	- "block configure" default of -layoutdepth is -1, as documented;
	- "block data" is available as a method (blockData);
	- Tcl_IntResults with a single value aborts in Tk 804; replaced.
	Fixes after a review of the port (t/regress.t covers them):
	- "tag prevrange" crashed whenever the last toggle of the tag was
	  not on the first line of its B-tree node (NULL dereference, or an
	  index past the end of the line); the backward search is reworked:
	  only lines of the outermost tree count against its line budget,
	  it leaves a block into the enclosing line, and compares the stop
	  position with external offsets.  Verified against "tag ranges"
	  for every index/stop position in a dozen block layouts.  (Where
	  Tk's own text widget returns a range starting before index2,
	  contrary to its documentation, eText follows the documentation.)
	- "search -nocase": the lower-casing of the extracted line wrote
	  through a pointer taken before the buffer was appended to (which
	  reallocates it under Perl/Tk): heap corruption on any line longer
	  than the initial buffer.
	- A layout callback returning a single row (all an -empty block
	  needs) was rejected ("List of wrong size").
	- "block delete" / widget destruction did not release the block's
	  callbacks and -layoutwidths (a -layoutcmd closure kept the widget
	  alive); tag -data was never released; "dump -command" given twice
	  leaked the first callback; "block list" could leak a pending value
	  before an embedded window, and returned the block instance data
	  by alias (modifying the result modified the block).
	- "window cget -align" died with "Modification of a read-only
	  value" (and printed "centre" for "center"); "tag bind" queried
	  without a binding croaked with an empty message; "dump" read past
	  the argument vector when the last argument began with "-";
	  "search -nocase" leaked the pattern copy on an invalid index.
	- A display line too narrow for one character split a multi-byte
	  character (whole UTF-8 characters are forced now, as in Tk 8.4).
	- Build: MakeMaker ran eTextSyms.PL during every "make" and, on a
	  fresh checkout, replaced the symbol-prefix header by an empty one
	  (so the next build would have clashed with Tk's text widget); the
	  script is no longer run automatically, is idempotent, and refuses
	  to write an empty header.  One uncast allocation that GCC 14
	  rejects.  Dead Tcl-only compatibility macros removed.
	- editXxx/imageXxx methods now reach the explicit "not implemented"
	  message; the demo honours $ENV{kprefix}; POD file references.
	Features of the modern Tk text widget, added later in 2.00 (each
	with a test that compares the behaviour with Tk's own text widget
	on the same text where that is possible):
	- text indices count characters, not bytes (Perl/Tk 804 stores
	  text as UTF-8): the Tk 8.4 design - byte offsets inside, the
	  conversion at the "line.char" boundary - extended to count into
	  blocks (the dummy position at the start of a block is one
	  character); search -count and -nocase, dump, "block of", bbox,
	  wordstart/wordend follow.  A match inside a block was reported
	  one position too far (TkIBTreeTextAppend).  t/utf8.t.
	- the -elide tag option: hidden text takes no room, inside blocks
	  too (a hidden block disappears, hidden text inside a block
	  narrows it).  search does not skip elided text.  t/elide.t.
	- the image subcommand (embedded images, Tk 8.4's tkTextImage.c);
	  images work inside blocks and appear in "block list" as
	  Tk::Text::EmbImage; "dump -image" reports the image's name in
	  the widget, as documented.  t/image.t.
	- the edit subcommand (undo, redo, reset, separator, modified),
	  -undo, -maxundo, -autoseparators, <<Modified>>; the stack
	  (tkTextUndo.c) records the changes themselves and the widget
	  applies them with its own procedures.  The text of a deleted
	  block comes back as text when undone.  <<Undo>> and <<Redo>> are
	  bound.  (Perl/Tk's Tk::Text records nothing: "edit undo" there
	  always says "nothing to undo".)  t/edit.t.
	- the demo's TeX symbol tables (utils/Text/TeX.pm) give Unicode
	  characters instead of codes in 8-bit X11 symbol fonts, with
	  reverse maps (%char2tex, %tex_by_char) for the TeX export; the
	  sample document demo/save.tsf is converted; Save/Load use UTF-8.
	  t/tex.t, t/demo.t (which drives the demo under X).
	Fixes and additions after that:
	- "tag nextrange" missed a range starting at index1 inside a block
	  (it looked for the on-toggle in the outer line); the forward tag
	  search compared a stop position inside a block with internal
	  offsets (TkBTreeNextTag, the "XXXX Wrong" of 1997); TkTextRedrawTag
	  handed internal indices to the display-line search.  t/regress.t
	  sweeps nextrange over the block layouts too (6,456 queries).
	- search leaves elided text out unless -elide is given, and so does
	  the selection, as in Tk 8.4 (the line extraction skips elided
	  segments and blocks under TKT_GET_NOELIDE); search -count counts
	  the characters of the matched text.
	- "+N lines" keeps the character position (Tk keeps the byte offset,
	  TK_BUGS.md A5).
	- dump looks into blocks: the elements inside a block are reported
	  with external indices, between "block" and "blockend" elements
	  (the -block switch, part of -all).  t/dump.t.
	- undo re-creates the blocks (leaves, nesting, instance data), tags
	  and marks of a deleted range that does not cut a block, from what
	  "block list" reports, with the tag toggles where they were; a
	  deletion that cuts a block (which the widget dissolves) comes back
	  as text; windows and images are not re-created.  "block data"
	  accepts the start position of an empty or top-level block.  The
	  demo has -undo on: Control-z undoes, formulas included.
	- TK_BUGS.md: A4 (search -nocase and case-changing lengths), A5,
	  B6 (booleans), C4 (Tk::Text's undo records nothing), C5 (dump
	  -image), D5.
	The next batch:
	- undo of a deletion that cut blocks re-creates them.  Such a
	  deletion is recorded as the "block list" of the smallest range
	  around it that cuts no block, with the size of what the
	  dissolution of the cut blocks leaves behind (TkIBTreeCutRange);
	  the undo takes those remains out and re-inserts the list, redo
	  repeats the original deletion.  Found on the way: a right-gravity
	  mark in a re-created range drifted to the end of the insertion
	  (text inserted at an index goes before the right-gravity marks
	  there; such marks get their gravity back only at the end now);
	  deleting all of a block but its head, or all but its tail (which
	  deletes the block, as documented), crashed: TkBTreeKillBlockProc
	  freed the lone newline of an emptied block while the outer line
	  still linked to it, and TkBTreeDeleteChars rebalanced from a line
	  of the destroyed block.  t/edit.t (a dozen layouts: the text,
	  "dump -all" and the block list before and after), t/regress.t.
	- the "display" and "any" index modifiers of Tk 8.5: "+N display
	  indices" (or chars) steps over elided text, "display wordstart"
	  and "display wordend" see the visible characters only ("any"
	  means the plain modifier; "indices" is accepted as a synonym of
	  "chars").  The widget's cursor keys (Left, Right, Home, End,
	  the word keys, the Emacs ones) use them, so
	  the insertion cursor skips elided text, as in Tk 8.5, where
	  Tk::Text's can sit in it.  Tk 8.6's "display wordend" walks into
	  elided text (TK_BUGS.md A6); etext's does not.  t/elide.t (every
	  position of a text with elided ranges, compared with wish if it
	  is installed; blocks; the keys).
	- undo re-creates embedded images and windows (Tk's text widget
	  restores neither): the Tk::Text::EmbImage and Tk::Text::EmbWin
	  elements of "block list" carry the options as option/value
	  pairs, and the undo creates the segment again through the image
	  or window command.  An image whose Tk image is gone comes back
	  empty; a window given with -window was destroyed with the
	  deletion (as in Tk) and comes back as an empty window segment,
	  one with -create is created again when displayed; either way
	  the segment keeps its unit of index space, so the recorded
	  indices of the other changes stay right.  This covers images
	  and windows among the remains of a cut block.  t/edit.t.
	- an embedded window inside a block can be addressed by name and
	  by index: "index" by the window's name gave the position inside
	  the block, and "window cget"/"window configure" could not find
	  the segment there.  t/regress.t.
	- "display linestart", "display lineend" and "+N display lines"
	  (Tk 8.5): the display line containing an index is found by
	  laying the logical line out from its start; "display lines"
	  keeps the x position, as the Up and Down keys do, passes over
	  entirely elided lines and stops at the last display line of
	  the text (Tk 8.6's answer past the end depends on the x
	  position, TK_BUGS.md A7).  Display lines never span logical
	  lines here: a line whose newline is elided still ends a display
	  line (Tk 8.5 merges it with the next one; the answers differ
	  only around an elided newline), and the widget wraps as Tk 8.4
	  does (a space at the right margin needs a pixel of room, Tk 8.5
	  lets it hang over).  Home and End go to the first and the last
	  visible position of the display line (Tk 8.5).  t/elide.t:
	  every position of a wrapped text with elided ranges compared
	  with wish 8.6.14 for display linestart, lineend and -2..2
	  display lines (1126 queries), after checking that the two wrap
	  alike.

